utput schema, deterministic validation, cross-checks against known data and a review queue for anything uncertain."], ["Should the model post directly to the core system?", "No. The model should only produce a candidate record. Deterministic code validates it and posts it, so the model never holds credentials or decides what is written."], ["How do you set confidence thresholds?", "Measure against a labelled sample of real documents, per document type and per field, and set thresholds from observed error rates rather than the model's self-reported certainty."]], "related": ["/ai-architect/", "/insights/structured-outputs-reliable-llm-integration/", "/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/"], "citations": [{"title": "JSON Schema specification", "url": "https://json-schema.org/specification", "publisher": "JSON Schema"}, {"title": "LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection", "url": "https://genai.owasp.org/llmrisk/llm01-prompt-injection/", "publisher": "OWASP Gen AI Security Project"}, {"title": "Constraints (PostgreSQL documentation)", "url": "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html", "publisher": "PostgreSQL Global Development Group"}, {"title": "AI Risk Management Framework", "url": "https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework", "publisher": "NIST"}]}-->
<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A property operator onboarded tenants and paid suppliers through a core property management system. Everything that fed it arrived as documents: signed leases, identity documents, proof of address, supplier invoices and service contracts. Each was opened, read and keyed by hand. Onboarding waited on the queue, keying errors surfaced later as billing disputes, and the team spent its time transcribing rather than checking.</p>
<p>Earlier attempts with template-based OCR had struggled because the documents varied too much: different lease templates, scanned and photographed IDs, and invoices in every layout a supplier could invent.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Every value validated before it reaches the core system</strong>, by rules the business can read.</li>
<li><strong>The model never writes</strong>: it proposes a record, and deterministic code decides what is posted.</li>
<li><strong>Uncertain documents go to people</strong>, with the reason and the source region visible.</li>
<li><strong>Personal data handled deliberately</strong>: limited access, defined retention and no identity documents in logs or prompts beyond what extraction needs.</li>
<li><strong>Traceability</strong> from any posted value back to the page it came from.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<p>Each document type had a versioned JSON Schema describing its fields, types, formats and required properties<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The model was asked to return output conforming to that schema, and the same schema validated the response, so a malformed answer failed loudly instead of slipping through. More on the pattern in <a href="/insights/structured-outputs-reliable-llm-integration/">structured outputs for reliable LLM integration</a>.</p>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Capture</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Tenant portal<em>uploads</em></span><span>Shared mailbox<em>invoices</em></span><span>Branch scanning<em>signed leases</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Classify</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Document classifier<em>type and pages</em></span><span>Object storage<em>original, immutable</em></span><span>PII controls<em>access and retention</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Extract</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Vision LLM<em>schema-constrained output</em></span><span>Schema registry<em>versioned per type</em></span><span>Source regions<em>page and box per field</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Validate</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Rules and cross-checks<em>totals, dates, references</em></span><span>Confidence scoring<em>per field</em></span><span class="hl">Exception queue<em>reason-coded</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Post</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Workflow engine<em>idempotent steps</em></span><span>Core system API<em>service account</em></span><span>Audit store<em>Postgres</em></span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Layered architecture: the model extracts, rules decide, and only deterministic code holds the credentials to post.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Receive</b><span>Store the original unchanged; record channel, uploader and checksum</span></li>
<li><b>Classify</b><span>Identify document type and split multi-document uploads</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Extract</b><span>Vision model returns a schema-shaped record with a source region for each field</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Validate</b><span>Schema check, business rules, and cross-checks against tenant, unit and supplier data</span></li>
<li><b>Route</b><span>Auto-post when every field passes thresholds; otherwise queue with reason codes</span></li>
<li><b>Post</b><span>Workflow engine writes to the core system once, with an idempotency key, and logs lineage</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Each step is recorded, so a disputed value can be traced to the document, model version and reviewer.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The audit and staging tables in Postgres carried their own check, not-null and foreign key constraints, so invalid states were rejected by the database as well as by the application<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Retries were safe because posting was idempotent, the same discipline described in <a href="/insights/idempotent-payments-order-pipelines/">idempotent payment and order pipelines</a>.</p>

<h2>The hard part: confidence you can act on</h2>
<p>Extraction quality was rarely the blocker. Deciding when to trust it was. A model's own statement of certainty is not a reliable signal, so confidence was built from evidence instead:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Arithmetic and consistency checks</strong>: invoice lines must sum to totals, lease dates must be in order, rent schedules must match the term.</li>
<li><strong>Reference checks</strong>: the unit, tenant or supplier must exist and match what the document claims.</li>
<li><strong>Field-level thresholds</strong> set per document type from a labelled sample, with stricter rules on fields that move money or identify people.</li>
<li><strong>Reason codes</strong> on every exception, so the team could see which rule or field caused most reviews and fix the cause.</li>
</ul>
<p>Documents are also untrusted input. Text inside an uploaded file can carry instructions aimed at the model, a form of indirect prompt injection<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. The defence was architectural: the extraction model had no tools and no credentials, its output was confined to the schema, and posting was done by code that ignored anything outside defined fields. See <a href="/insights/prompt-injection-defence-for-agents/">prompt injection defence</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>The model is the least trusted component in the pipeline. Design so that the worst it can do is produce a record that fails validation.</p></div>

<h2>How the engagement ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Pipeline</small><ol><li>Classify and extract</li><li class="hl">Validate and score</li><li>Auto-post or queue</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Operations reviewer</small><ol><li>Open queued document</li><li class="hl">Correct highlighted fields</li><li>Approve for posting</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Process owner</small><ol><li>Review reason-code trends</li><li>Adjust rules and thresholds</li><li>Approve schema changes</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Corrections feed the evaluation set; reason-code trends feed the rules.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Discover</b><ul><li>Document inventory</li><li>Field and rule catalogue</li><li>Data protection review</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>One document type</b><ul><li>Schema and extraction</li><li>Labelled sample</li><li>Review UI</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Harden</b><ul><li>Cross-checks and thresholds</li><li>Idempotent posting</li><li>Monitoring</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Extend and hand off</b><ul><li>Further document types</li><li>Runbooks</li><li>Change process for schemas</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Phased delivery: start with one high-volume document type and add others on the same rails.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Ongoing monitoring of exception rates, corrections and drift by document type followed the measure-and-manage approach of the NIST AI Risk Management Framework<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Vision LLM with schemas</td><td>Template OCR per layout</td><td>Handles layout variety without a template for every supplier</td></tr>
<tr><td>Deterministic posting</td><td>Agent with system access</td><td>Model holds no credentials; injection cannot cause writes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Evidence-based confidence</td><td>Model self-reported scores</td><td>Thresholds grounded in checks and measured error rates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reason-coded exceptions</td><td>Generic review queue</td><td>Shows which rule or field to improve next</td></tr>
<tr><td>Versioned schemas per type</td><td>One generic document schema</td><td>Clear validation and safe, reviewable change</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: keep the model away from credentials and let validation, not the model, decide what is posted. Differently: build the labelled sample before the pipeline. Every threshold discussion went faster once there was a shared set of real documents to measure against.</p>
<p>If you're planning something similar, the <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architect</a> page describes how I run these engagements, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
